If it is lower, we get aliasing

5. Data conversion and quantization
Data conversion changes a signal from analog to digital, or from digital to analog.
Quantization means dividing a continuous analog signal into fixed digital steps or levels.


6. Sample-and-hold circuits
A sample-and-hold circuit is an electronic circuit. This circuit takes the signal value at one moment (sample) and keeps it constant for a short time (hold).

7. Digital-to-analog conversion (DAC)
Digital-to-analog conversion changes digital data into an analog signal. Digital data uses 0 and 1. For example, a computer stores music in digital form. A speaker changes this digital information into sound. This process is called DAC.

8. Analog-to-Digital Conversion
Analog-to-digital conversion changes an analog signal into digital data. First, the system takes samples from the signal. Then, it changes these samples into digital numbers.

9. Discretization of Continuous-Time Processes
A continuous-time signal changes all the time. Discretization means taking samples from this signal at different times   
 

10. Reconstruction of continuous functions
This is the opposite of discretization. We make a continuous function from the samples.
•	Zero-order hold: keeps each value constant (looks like steps).
•	First-order hold: connects the points with straight lines.





11. Fourier Transform
The Fourier transform shows which frequencies are inside a signal. It changes a signal from the time domain to the frequency domain. Example: a sound has many tones. The Fourier transform shows each tone.
 

12. Modified Z-transform	
The Modified Z-transform is a method used to study digital systems. It helps us understand how a system works with digital signals and delays.

13. Analog Model of the Extrapolator
An extrapolator is a system that predicts future signal values. The analog model works with continuous signals. It is used in control and communication systems. It helps predict signal changes.

14. Stable and unstable systems
A stable system works correctly and gives limited output for limited input. An unstable system can produce a very large output. It may not work correctly.
Example: H(z) = 1/(z − 0.5) is stable. H(z) = 1/(z − 2) is unstable.

15. Design of Digital Filters and BIBO
A digital filter changes a signal. It removes noise or keeps some frequencies. Types: low-pass, high-pass, band-pass, band-stop.
•	FIR filter: no feedback. Always stable. Can have linear phase.
•	IIR filter: uses feedback. Needs fewer coefficients, but can be unstable.
BIBO means Bounded Input, Bounded Output. If the input is limited, the output must also be limited.
16. Stability of Discrete-Time Systems and Routh-Hurwitz Criterion
Stability means that the system gives a limited output. The Routh-Hurwitz criterion is a mathematical method for checking stability.

17. Sampling and Quantization – Convolution
Sampling takes values from a signal at different times. Quantization changes these values into fixed levels. Convolution is a mathematical operation between two signals. It helps us understand the output of a system.

18. Nyquist Criterion for Stability
The Nyquist criterion checks the stability of a closed-loop system using the open-loop frequency response G(jω)H(jω).
•	Formula: Z = N + P 
o	Z = number of unstable closed-loop poles
o	P = number of unstable open-loop poles
o	N = number of clockwise turns around −1
•	The system is stable if Z = 0.


19. Digital Controller – D/A Conversion
A digital controller controls a system using digital data. It receives information, processes it and sends a control signal. D/A conversion changes digital data into an analog signal. For example, a computer can control a motor using a digital controller and a D/A converter.

