29. Design of IIR Filter Using Bilinear Z-Transform
First, we choose an analog filter. Then, we find its transfer function. We use the bilinear transform formula to change the analog filter into a digital filter. We replace s with z. Finally, we get a digital filter.


30. FIR and IIR Filters
FIR and IIR are two types of digital filters. FIR means Finite Impulse Response. It uses only previous input values. IIR means Infinite Impulse Response. It uses previous input and output values. FIR filters are always stable in normal conditions, but IIR filters can be stable or unstable. Both are used to remove noise and improve signals.



31. Recursive Smoother
A smoother reduces noise and makes a signal more smooth. A recursive smoother uses the old output to make the new output (feedback).
•	Simple formula (exponential smoother): y(n) = α·x(n) + (1 − α)·y(n−1), where 0 < α < 1.
It is used in digital signal processing.

32. Group Delay, Linear Phase, All-pass, Minimum Phase
Group delay shows how much a signal is delayed by a filter. Linear phase means that all frequencies have the same time delay. An all-pass filter changes the phase but keeps the signal size the same. A minimum-phase filter has a small delay. These filters are used in digital signal processing.


33. Analog-to-Digital Conversion
Analog-to-digital conversion changes an analog signal into digital data. First, the system takes samples from the signal. Then, it changes these samples into digital numbers. For example, a microphone changes sound into digital data. This process is called ADC.
•	Steps: Anti-aliasing filter → Sampling → Hold → Quantization → Encoding.


34. A Fundamental Definition of System Stability
System stability means that a system works correctly. A stable system gives a limited output when it receives a limited input. An unstable system can give a very large output. 
Tests: Routh-Hurwitz, Jury test, Nyquist.

35. Sampling and Quantization
Sampling takes values from a signal at different times. Quantization changes these values into fixed levels. These processes change an analog signal into digital data. They are important in digital systems. They are used in sound and image processing.

36. Aliasing and Anti-Aliasing in Digital Systems
Aliasing happens when a signal is sampled too slowly. It happens when fs < 2·fmax. It can change the real information of a signal. Anti-aliasing helps prevent this problem. It uses a special filter(low-pass filter ) before sampling. This makes the digital signal more accurate.


37. Nyquist–Shannon Sampling Theorem
The Nyquist-Shannon theorem explains how to sample a signal correctly. The sampling frequency must be at least two times higher than the maximum signal frequency. This helps us get the original signal back. It also helps prevent aliasing.


38. Correlation Analysis
Correlation analysis shows the relationship between two signals. It helps us understand how similar they are. If two signals change in the same direction, they have positive correlation. If they change in opposite directions, they have negative correlation. It is used in signal processing and data analysis.


39. Equalization Filters
An equalization filter changes the frequency parts of a signal. It can increase or decrease some frequencies. It helps improve sound quality.
Types: 
•	Linear (FIR) equalizer, Adaptive equalizer, Decision feedback equalizer (DFE)

40. Frequency Response and Transfer Function
Frequency response shows how a system works at different frequencies. It has two parts: Magnitude , Phase.
 A transfer function shows the relationship between input and output  H(z) = Y(z)/X(z).  

41. Quantization Error
Quantization error is the difference between the real signal value and the digital value. It happens during quantization. A computer cannot save every signal value exactly. This creates a small error. We try to make this error smaller.


42. All-Pass Filters
An all-pass filter is a digital filter. It allows all frequencies to pass. It changes the phase of a signal but keeps its size the same. It is used in audio and digital signal processing. It helps control signal delay.

43. The Fourier Transform and Digital Fourier Transform
The Fourier Transform changes a signal from the time domain to the frequency domain. It helps us see the frequencies in a signal. The Digital Fourier Transform works with digital signals. It is used to study sound, music and images. It is important in signal processing.


44. First-Order Notch Filter
A first-order notch filter reduces a special frequency in a signal. It allows other frequencies to pass. It helps remove unwanted noise. For example, it can reduce noise in audio signals. It is used in electronic systems.

45. Second-Order Unity Gain Resonator
A second-order unity gain resonator is a type of digital filter. It works with a special frequency. Unity gain means that the signal size stays the same at a selected frequency. It is used in audio and signal processing. It helps control and study frequencies.

46. State Space
State Space is a mathematical method for describing a system. It uses input, output and state variables. It helps us understand how a system works over time. Engineers use it to study and control systems. It is important in control engineering.

47. Transformation of Random Signals in Discrete-Time Systems
Random signals are signals that change in an unpredictable way. A discrete-time system takes samples of these signals. Then, it processes the samples. The output signal may also be random. This process is used in communication and digital signal processing.

48. High-Pass Filters
A high-pass filter allows high frequencies to pass. It reduces low frequencies. It can remove low-frequency noise from a signal. It is used in audio and communication systems. It helps improve signal quality.

49. Low-Pass Filters
A low-pass filter allows low frequencies to pass. It reduces high frequencies. It can remove unwanted high-frequency noise. It is used in audio and digital systems. It helps make signals smoother.

50. Band-Pass Filters
A band-pass filter allows a selected range of frequencies to pass. It reduces very low and very high frequencies. It is used in radio and communication systems. It helps select useful signals. It is important in digital signal processing.


51. Relationship Between Sampling Frequency and Maximum Frequency
Sampling frequency shows how many samples we take in one second. The maximum frequency is the highest frequency in a signal. According to the Nyquist theorem, the sampling frequency must be at least two times higher than the maximum frequency. This helps us get the original signal back.


52. Nyquist Frequency, Aliasing and Signal Reconstruction
Nyquist frequency is half of the sampling frequency. Aliasing happens when we sample a signal too slowly. It changes the real frequency information. Signal reconstruction means creating the original signal from digital samples. We need a correct sampling frequency to avoid aliasing.
