54. Fundamental Principles of Digital System Analysis
Digital system analysis helps us understand how a digital system works. It studies input, output and system behavior. It also studies stability and frequency response. Engineers use mathematical methods to analyze systems. This helps them design better digital systems.


55. Role of the Z-Transform in Analyzing Discrete-Time Systems
The Z-transform is a mathematical method. It changes a discrete-time signal into the Z-domain. It helps us study digital systems. We can find the transfer function, poles and zeros. It also helps us check system stability.

56. Linearity, Time Invariance, Causality and Stability
Linearity means that a system follows the rules of addition and multiplication. Time invariance means that the system works in the same way at different times. Causality means that the output depends only on present and past inputs. Stability means that a limited input gives a limited output. These properties help us understand digital systems.
57. Poles and Zeros in Digital Systems
Poles and zeros are important parts of a transfer function. Zeros are points where the transfer function becomes zero. Poles are points where the transfer function becomes very large. They affect the system's behavior and frequency response. They also help us check system stability.


